  "game_over": {
    "seq": 228,
    "message": "Player GPT4om Stoic is the winner",
  },
  "youtube_url": "https://youtu.be/gi7ZjrzrVB8",
  "annotations": [
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Playing Cavern of Souls on turn 1 is suboptimal because it only produces colorless mana or one color for a chosen creature type. The hand has Bringer of the Last Gift (Vampire Demon) and Superior Spider-Man (Spider Human Hero) — these share no creature type, so Cavern can only help cast one of them. More importantly, Starting Town enters untapped on turns 1-3 and produces any color of mana (at 1 life cost), making it ideal for later turns when you need flexible colored mana. Playing Starting Town now while it's free to enter untapped, and saving Cavern of Souls for later when you know which creature type to name, would be better sequencing.",
      "action_taken": "Played Cavern of Souls on turn 1",
      "better_line": "Play Starting Town on turn 1 (enters untapped on turns 1-3 for free), saving Cavern of Souls for a later turn when the creature type choice is more informed. Alternatively, Breeding Pool or Overgrown Tomb could be played paying 2 life to enter untapped, providing colored mana immediately.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 3,
      "player": "Haiku Libby",
      "decision_index": 5,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Cavern of Souls was named 'Avatar' but Bringer of the Last Gift is a Vampire Demon, not an Avatar. The two Superior Spider-Man cards are Spider Human Hero creatures. By naming Avatar, the Cavern of Souls can't produce colored mana for any creature in hand and its 'can't be countered' clause won't apply to anything relevant. Naming 'Human' or 'Spider' would have covered the two Superior Spider-Man copies, and naming 'Vampire' or 'Demon' would have covered Bringer of the Last Gift.",
      "action_taken": "Named 'Avatar' for Cavern of Souls",
      "better_line": "Name 'Demon' or 'Vampire' to cast Bringer of the Last Gift uncounterably with colored mana, or name 'Human' or 'Spider' to support the two Superior Spider-Man copies. Given that Bringer costs 6BB and is the hardest to cast and most impactful card, naming Demon or Vampire would likely be best.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 3,
      "player": "Haiku Libby",
      "decision_index": 5,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Playing Multiversal Passage costs 2 life to enter untapped when a basic Mountain would enter untapped for free and accomplish the same thing (enabling Fear of Missing Out cast this turn). Multiversal Passage should be saved for a later turn when its flexibility is more valuable.",
      "action_taken": "Played Multiversal Passage (paying 2 life to enter untapped)",
      "better_line": "Play basic Mountain (enters untapped for free), saving Multiversal Passage for a later turn when the basic land type matters more or when other untapped land options are exhausted.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 15,
      "player": "GPT4om Stoic",
      "decision_index": 14,
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Fear of Missing Out's enter-the-battlefield trigger requires discarding a card then drawing a card. Discarding Boomerang Basics (a sorcery) adds a sorcery type to the graveyard, which helps enable Delirium (4+ card types in graveyard). However, Boomerang Basics is a useful tempo/removal spell that can bounce opposing threats. The player could have discarded a land instead (like Riverpyre Verge or Mountain, since they already have Spirebluff Canal and Multiversal Passage plus will draw a card). Discarding a land would add the land type to graveyard for Delirium while preserving the more flexible spell. That said, discarding Boomerang Basics does add a sorcery type which may be harder to get otherwise, and the player may value having more land drops. This is debatable.",
      "action_taken": "Discarded Boomerang Basics to Fear of Missing Out's ETB trigger",
      "better_line": "Discarding a redundant land like Riverpyre Verge or Mountain would preserve the flexible bounce spell while still contributing a card type to the graveyard for Delirium. The player already has two lands in play and multiple lands in hand.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 18,
      "player": "GPT4om Stoic",
      "decision_index": 15,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Cast Oblivious Bookworm before making a land drop on turn 3. By playing a land first (e.g., Starting Town, which enters untapped on turn 3, or Overgrown Tomb paying 2 life), the player would have 3 mana available after casting the 2-mana Bookworm, leaving mana open. Playing spells before lands is a sequencing error that reduces flexibility.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Oblivious Bookworm before playing a land for the turn.",
      "better_line": "Play Starting Town (enters untapped on turn 3) or Overgrown Tomb first, then cast Oblivious Bookworm, leaving an extra mana available.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 22,
      "player": "Haiku Libby",
      "decision_index": 17,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Playing Overgrown Tomb (paying 2 life to enter untapped) when Starting Town would enter untapped for free on turn 3 and produce the same colors needed this turn. Starting Town only costs 1 life for colored mana vs 2 life for Overgrown Tomb entering untapped.",
      "action_taken": "Played Overgrown Tomb, paying 2 life to have it enter untapped",
      "better_line": "Play Starting Town (enters untapped on turn 3 for free), tap it for {G} (1 life) and Breeding Pool for {U} to cast Oblivious Bookworm, saving 1 life. Save Overgrown Tombs for later turns when permanent {B} sources are needed.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 23,
      "player": "Haiku Libby",
      "decision_index": 19,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "GPT4om Stoic cast Fear of Missing Out before playing a land for the turn. They have multiple lands in hand (Island, Riverpyre Verge, Steam Vents, Mountain, Spirebluff Canal) and should play a land first to ensure they don't miss a land drop. More importantly, Fear of Missing Out's enter-the-battlefield trigger requires discarding a card then drawing - by casting it before playing a land, they risk discarding a land they could have played, or they lose flexibility in choosing what to discard since they haven't committed to a land drop yet. Additionally, casting a creature before combat when you already have a Fear of Missing Out on the battlefield is slightly questionable - though there's no combat trick concern here. The main issue is sequencing: land should come before spells.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Fear of Missing Out before playing a land for the turn.",
      "better_line": "Play a land first (likely Steam Vents paying 2 life, or Spirebluff Canal which would enter tapped as the 4th land), then cast Fear of Missing Out. This preserves maximum flexibility for the discard/draw trigger and ensures no missed land drop.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 28,
      "player": "GPT4om Stoic",
      "decision_index": 21,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Discarded Mountain instead of the second Spirebluff Canal. With 2 lands already in play, any future Spirebluff Canal would enter tapped (requires 2 or fewer other lands), making it strictly worse than a basic Mountain which always enters untapped. Mountain provides red mana just like Spirebluff Canal would.",
      "action_taken": "Discarded Mountain",
      "better_line": "Discard Spirebluff Canal, which will always enter tapped from this point forward, while Mountain always enters untapped.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 29,
      "player": "GPT4om Stoic",
      "decision_index": 23,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Playing Island instead of Spirebluff Canal on turn 3 is a sequencing error. With 2 other lands in play, Spirebluff Canal enters untapped now, but on turn 4 with 3 other lands it would enter tapped. Island always enters untapped so should be saved for later. Additionally, playing Island this turn provides only U mana when the other two lands are tapped, while Spirebluff Canal would provide R, enabling Torch the Tower if needed.",
      "action_taken": "Played Island as the turn 3 land drop",
      "better_line": "Play Spirebluff Canal, which enters untapped now (2 or fewer other lands) but would enter tapped next turn. This also provides R mana to enable Torch the Tower this turn if needed.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 29,
      "player": "GPT4om Stoic",
      "decision_index": 23,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Opponent has zero creatures on the battlefield, making attacking with all creatures completely free. By only attacking with one Fear of Missing Out (2 damage), GPT4om Stoic left 5 additional damage on the table (second FoMO for 2 + Tiger-Seal for 3 with vigilance so it stays untapped).",
      "action_taken": "Attacked with only one Fear of Missing Out for 2 damage.",
      "better_line": "Attack with both Fear of Missing Out and Tiger-Seal for 7 total damage against an empty board, bringing opponent from 16 to 9.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 33,
      "player": "GPT4om Stoic",
      "decision_index": 24,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Casting Formidable Speaker before playing a land is a sequencing error. Haiku Libby has Overgrown Tomb and Starting Town in hand, and should play a land first to have 4 mana available. With 4 mana, they could potentially cast Formidable Speaker and still have mana open, or play the land untapped (Starting Town on turn 4 enters tapped, but Overgrown Tomb could enter untapped for 2 life). More importantly, playing the land first is free and preserves options - for instance, if Formidable Speaker's ETB trigger lets them discard and tutor, having the extra land on board could matter for subsequent plays. Additionally, casting creatures before combat is generally suboptimal when you have nothing to gain from it pre-combat, though here the opponent has a tapped Fear of Missing Out and Tiger-Seal (which taps on upkeep) so combat isn't particularly relevant.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Formidable Speaker before playing a land drop",
      "better_line": "Play Overgrown Tomb (paying 2 life to have it enter untapped) first, then cast Formidable Speaker, preserving the extra mana for potential plays after the tutor effect resolves",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 38,
      "player": "Haiku Libby",
      "decision_index": 28,
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Discarded Oblivious Bookworm, a strong card advantage engine, instead of a redundant second Superior Spider-Man. Spider-Man is legendary, so only one can be on the battlefield at a time, making the second copy less valuable. Bookworm provides ongoing card draw which would help find lands and gas. The player also incorrectly believed they already had a Bookworm in play.",
      "action_taken": "Discarded Oblivious Bookworm to Formidable Speaker's tutor ability",
      "better_line": "Discard the second Superior Spider-Man since it's legendary and redundant while keeping Oblivious Bookworm for card advantage",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 39,
      "player": "Haiku Libby",
      "decision_index": 30,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Casting Stormchaser's Talent before playing a land drop is a sequencing error. GPT4om Stoic has three lands in hand (Riverpyre Verge, Steam Vents, Spirebluff Canal) and should play a land first before casting spells. Playing a land first costs nothing and preserves information — you might want to hold the Talent depending on what develops. More importantly, the Otter token created by Stormchaser's Talent has prowess, so casting spells after the token enters would trigger prowess and pump it. By casting the Talent now (before combat), the token won't benefit from any prowess triggers this turn unless additional spells are cast. However, with only 1 untapped mana remaining after casting Talent (Island), there's limited ability to cast more spells. The land-before-spell sequencing is the main issue.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Stormchaser's Talent before playing a land for the turn.",
      "better_line": "Play a land first (e.g., Spirebluff Canal or Steam Vents), then cast Stormchaser's Talent. This preserves proper sequencing and keeps options open.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 47,
      "player": "GPT4om Stoic",
      "decision_index": 35,
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Chose Jeskai (draw on combat damage) over Temur (+1/+0, trample, haste) when opponent has no creatures and is at 12 life. Temur would add +1 power to each creature per combat, which compounds with Fear of Missing Out's extra combat phases. Against an empty board, the extra damage from Temur likely closes the game faster than drawing 1 card per combat phase.",
      "action_taken": "Chose Jeskai mode for Frostcliff Siege",
      "better_line": "Choose Temur mode to maximize damage output against an opponent with no blockers, leveraging the +1/+0 across multiple combat steps from Fear of Missing Out's delirium trigger",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 60,
      "player": "GPT4om Stoic",
      "decision_index": 44,
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Spirebluff Canal enters tapped with 4+ other lands, while Steam Vents could enter untapped (for 2 life). Since both lands will be available eventually, the question is whether having an extra untapped mana this turn is worth 2 life. With two Fear of Missing Out on board ready to attack and potential need for mana flexibility this turn, playing Steam Vents untapped might be slightly preferable.",
      "action_taken": "Played Spirebluff Canal (enters tapped)",
      "better_line": "Play Steam Vents (paying 2 life for untapped) to have maximum mana available this turn, saving Spirebluff Canal (which will always enter tapped) for a future turn when the tempo loss matters less.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 61,
      "player": "GPT4om Stoic",
      "decision_index": 45,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Discarded Requiting Hex, a 1-mana removal spell that can kill Tiger-Seal (3/3 vigilance), Fear of Missing Out, or Otter Token, while at 6 life facing an aggressive board. This is one of the most immediately impactful and castable cards in hand.",
      "action_taken": "Discarded Requiting Hex to hand size",
      "better_line": "Discard Starting Town (enters tapped after turn 3, and paying 1 life for colored mana is dangerous at 6 life) or a second copy of the legendary Superior Spider-Man. Keep Requiting Hex as cheap interaction to survive the aggressive board.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 70,
      "player": "Haiku Libby",
      "decision_index": 47,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Leveled up Stormchaser's Talent before playing a land. Should have played Island or Steam Vents first to have more mana available after the level-up, potentially allowing them to cast the recovered spell (Torch the Tower) in the same turn or hold up Spell Snare. With the opponent at 6 life, having Torch the Tower plus combat damage could be lethal this turn.",
      "action_taken": "Leveled up Stormchaser's Talent to level 2 before playing a land drop",
      "better_line": "Play Island (or Steam Vents) first, then level up Stormchaser's Talent to recover Torch the Tower, potentially enabling lethal with combat damage plus 2-3 burn damage to face (if Torch could target players - it can't, so recover Torch for creature removal). At minimum, having the extra land untapped provides more flexibility.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 73,
      "player": "GPT4om Stoic",
      "decision_index": 50,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Stormchaser's Talent level 2 triggers to return an instant or sorcery from the graveyard. With the opponent at 6 life, Torch the Tower is a much more impactful choice than Boomerang Basics. Torch the Tower can deal 2-3 damage to a creature or planeswalker and provides removal, while Boomerang Basics is a sorcery-speed bounce that only draws a card if bouncing your own permanent. Given the aggressive board state (two Fear of Missing Out, Otter token) and the opponent at 6 life, having Torch the Tower back gives more flexibility for removal or potentially enabling attacks through blockers. Boomerang Basics has very limited utility here since the opponent only has lands on board.",
      "action_taken": "Chose Boomerang Basics from graveyard with Stormchaser's Talent level 2 trigger",
      "better_line": "Choose Torch the Tower instead, which provides actual removal and could help push through the last points of damage against an opponent at 6 life",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 73,
      "player": "GPT4om Stoic",
      "decision_index": 50,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "GPT4om Stoic cast Boomerang Basics (a sorcery costing {U}) before playing a land for the turn. They have Steam Vents and Island in hand. Playing a land first (Island, which enters untapped for free) would preserve mana options and is strictly better sequencing. Additionally, Boomerang Basics returns a nonland permanent to its owner's hand - if targeting their own permanent they draw a card, but if targeting an opponent's permanent they don't. With the opponent at 6 life and GPT4om having two Fear of Missing Out and combat creatures, the question is whether Boomerang Basics is even the right play here versus developing the board. However, bouncing one of the opponent's lands or setting up for combat could be reasonable. The main issue is casting a spell before playing a land drop.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Boomerang Basics before playing a land for the turn.",
      "better_line": "Play Island (free, untapped) first, then cast Boomerang Basics, preserving flexibility and ensuring the land drop isn't missed.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 74,
      "player": "GPT4om Stoic",
      "decision_index": 51,
    }
